'' linux-arm64: a byte / short / boolean argument or result keeps its width, as on macOS, but
'' with NO 'zeroext' / 'signext': generic AAPCS64 leaves a narrow argument's upper bits
'' undefined and the callee extends, so clang writes 'define i8 @fc(i8, i16)' (clang 23 in the
'' Lima VM) and fbc's C is 'int8 F( int8, uint16 )'. The stack slot is then 8 bytes by LLVM's
'' generic rules, not Apple's natural-size packing. The running proof is build/aapcslane.sh's
'' linux-arm64 lane (prefixes p8bs, p8mix, p8int, nreg, and the narrow results). See ExtWord_
'' in src/codegen/abi.tiko; macOS's is darwin-narrow-args-macos-arm64.test.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: -target linux-arm64 --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/darwin-narrow-args.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-NOT: signext
'' EXPECT-NOT: zeroext
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare void @"c_take"(ptr, ptr, ptr, ptr, ptr, ptr, ptr, ptr, i8, i16, i8, i16, i8)
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare i16 @"c_ret"()
'' EXPECT-OUT: define i8 @"T_RET"(i64 %p0)
'' EXPECT-OUT: define void @"T_TAKE"(i8 %p0, i16 %p1, i8 %p2)
'' EXPECT-OUT: = call i8 @"T_RET"(i64 509)
'' EXPECT-OUT: i8 %t13, i16 %t10, i8 %t9, i16 %t8, i8 %t5)
'' EXPECT-OUT: call void %t12(i8 %t11, i16 %t8, i8 %t7)
